<pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I72b90abe81d435b8eed1b61f53ca5372bc00eef4" commit="283972f0a23f766be3fa576e52fbaa54a89443ed" commit_message="Create library for &quot;generic&quot; callbacks  This creates a location where we can put simple, generic callbacks that can be used by platforms that do not need certain custom callbacks to be created. The long-term goal is to reduce boilerplate code.  To start, we'll target the psu_cb which for some platforms simply returns an enum value to indicate if the platform uses a battery or not.  BUG=none BRANCH=none TEST=tested on paine with follow-up patch  Change-Id: I72b90abe81d435b8eed1b61f53ca5372bc00eef4 Signed-off-by: David Hendricks &lt;dhendrix@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/315743 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="315743" owner_email="dhendrix@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/mosys" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/mosys" ref="refs/changes/43/315743/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ifddb5daa9a9450c516dae6fbb30d6844cf658869" commit="65540266bde2adbc57c2662a30b0c7b6fe79f6ae" commit_message="Use generic PSU callbacks for some Chromeboxes  Stumpy, Kiev, and Beltino have no variants which use battery power, so this switches them to use the generic PSU callbacks so that we can remove the trivially simple boilerplate psu.c files.  BUG=none BRANCH=none TEST=compiled for x86  Change-Id: Ifddb5daa9a9450c516dae6fbb30d6844cf658869 Signed-off-by: David Hendricks &lt;dhendrix@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/315744 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="315744" owner_email="dhendrix@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/mosys" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/mosys" ref="refs/changes/44/315744/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="I61671a80f3fa71649fb5d7930bdb2f479fccc59f" commit="3c09ad848a015167c32825ecacb7cbad5f872009" commit_message="UPSTREAM: rhashtable: kill ht-&gt;shift atomic operations  Commit c0c09bfdc415 (&quot;rhashtable: avoid unnecessary wakeup for worker queue&quot;) changed ht-&gt;shift to be atomic, which is actually unnecessary.  Instead of leaving the current shift in the core rhashtable structure, it can be cached inside the individual bucket tables.  There, it will only be initialized once during a new table allocation in the shrink/expansion slow path, and from then onward it stays immutable for the rest of the bucket table liftime.  That allows shift to be non-atomic. The patch also moves hash_rnd management into the table setup. The rhashtable structure now consumes 3 instead of 4 cachelines.  Signed-off-by: Daniel Borkmann &lt;daniel@iogearbox.net&gt; Cc: Ying Xue &lt;ying.xue@windriver.com&gt; Acked-by: Thomas Graf &lt;tgraf@suug.ch&gt; Signed-off-by: David S. Miller &lt;davem@davemloft.net&gt; (cherry picked from commit a5b6846f9e1a080493210013385c28faecee36f0)  BUG=chromium:556861 TEST=builds all four kernel flavors based on chromeos-3.18 branch  Change-Id: I61671a80f3fa71649fb5d7930bdb2f479fccc59f Signed-off-by: Grant Grundler &lt;grundler@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/314187 Reviewed-by: Dmitry Torokhov &lt;dtor@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="314187" owner_email="grundler@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/87/314187/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="Id971128bfb6a05aeaf72c3bdb50b0396bdf9ae6b" commit="a44d9a627c89a129debb78957ad3bce66403f0e0" commit_message="UPSTREAM: rhashtable: Fix read-side crash during rehash  This patch fixes a typo rhashtable_lookup_compare where we fail to recompute the hash when looking up the new table.  This causes elements to be missed and potentially a crash during a resize.  Reported-by: Thomas Graf &lt;tgraf@suug.ch&gt; Signed-off-by: Herbert Xu &lt;herbert@gondor.apana.org.au&gt; Signed-off-by: David S. Miller &lt;davem@davemloft.net&gt; (cherry picked from commit 393619474ec0ba2a16dee12ec78fd43164f1e9b7)  BUG=chromium:556861 TEST=builds all four kernel flavors based on chromeos-3.18 branch  Change-Id: Id971128bfb6a05aeaf72c3bdb50b0396bdf9ae6b Signed-off-by: Grant Grundler &lt;grundler@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/314188 Reviewed-by: Dmitry Torokhov &lt;dtor@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="314188" owner_email="grundler@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/88/314188/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I795a61db36a11ee539693ceac41568b094317303" commit="02dcd554d60853f48b8a66486deb3562bd1554b9" commit_message="storm: Use board_id number to identify RAM config  This makes storm use the raw board_id (exported from coreboot) instead of the name of the platform to determine the memory config.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:47200 BRANCH=storm TEST=tested on arkham  Change-Id: I795a61db36a11ee539693ceac41568b094317303 Signed-off-by: David Hendricks &lt;dhendrix@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/312312 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="312312" owner_email="dhendrix@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/mosys" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/mosys" ref="refs/changes/12/312312/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="I5d212742a9a0f90d95bc52008d0545b5dde23ae0" commit="1fda0b3ee5b9a0ca7a24551ef9d284cff6a5e41c" commit_message="UPSTREAM: x86/tls: Don't validate lm in set_thread_area() after all  It turns out that there's a lurking ABI issue.  GCC, when compiling this in a 32-bit program:  struct user_desc desc = {  .entry_number    = idx,  .base_addr       = base,  .limit           = 0xfffff,  .seg_32bit       = 1,  .contents        = 0, /* Data, grow-up */  .read_exec_only  = 0,  .limit_in_pages  = 1,  .seg_not_present = 0,  .useable         = 0, };  will leave .lm uninitialized.  This means that anything in the kernel that reads user_desc.lm for 32-bit tasks is unreliable.  Revert the .lm check in set_thread_area().  The value never did anything in the first place.  Fixes: 0e58af4e1d21 (&quot;x86/tls: Disallow unusual TLS segments&quot;) Signed-off-by: Andy Lutomirski &lt;luto@amacapital.net&gt; Acked-by: Thomas Gleixner &lt;tglx@linutronix.de&gt; Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org # Only if 0e58af4e1d21 is backported Cc: Linus Torvalds &lt;torvalds@linux-foundation.org&gt; Link: http://lkml.kernel.org/r/d7875b60e28c512f6a6fc0baf5714d58e7eaadbb.1418856405.git.luto@amacapital.net Signed-off-by: Ingo Molnar &lt;mingo@kernel.org&gt; (cherry picked from commit 3fb2f4237bb452eb4e98f6a5dbd5a445b4fed9d0)  Change-Id: I5d212742a9a0f90d95bc52008d0545b5dde23ae0 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/316005 Commit-Ready: Haixia Shi &lt;hshi@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Haixia Shi &lt;hshi@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Luigi Semenzato &lt;semenzato@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="316005" owner_email="hshi@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/05/316005/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I806a78f59f8c8185be19763a52c4535754c7fbc0" commit="6b2d70ccae6da58818cb4988efc51022d9fd2f26" commit_message="android_xmlrpc_server: Add sig handler for XML RPC daemon  Port over the XmlRpcServer wrapper from Autotest into the android xml rpc daemon. The wrapper adds the required signal handlers to help cleanup the sl4a connections on exit. This has been copied over from client/cros/xmlrpc_server.py because the android xml rpc daemon is a standalone component without any external dependencies.  BUG=b:25695720 TEST=`test_that --board=panther android1758-wifi-test-station-2.cros       network_WiFi_RetryConnectHidden --args use_wpa_cli=false`  Change-Id: I806a78f59f8c8185be19763a52c4535754c7fbc0 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/315750 Commit-Ready: Roshan Pius &lt;rpius@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Roshan Pius &lt;rpius@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Christopher Wiley &lt;wiley@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Ang Li &lt;angli@google.com&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="315750" owner_email="rpius@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/50/315750/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ie57d79f20def861c22f6e2e023545a65825ab3b4" commit="9cbfdf3b368ff693b12d0200f7083a016d2e3323" commit_message="tcpc: add 2 bytes into TX byte count register  Add 2 bytes into the TX byte count register used in TCPC interface.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:48256 BRANCH=none TEST=load on glados and attach zinger, make sure PD negotiation successful.  Change-Id: Ie57d79f20def861c22f6e2e023545a65825ab3b4 Signed-off-by: Alec Berg &lt;alecaberg@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/315879 Reviewed-by: Vincent Palatin &lt;vpalatin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="315879" owner_email="alecaberg@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/79/315879/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I840a3c48ac2397b36581380ec9a2f645ebbd7119" commit="2f0c5e7abd2df5ada605899070b14fa11dff8245" commit_message="Add ToT-1 to kernel_usb suite schedule  BUG=None TEST=None  Change-Id: I840a3c48ac2397b36581380ec9a2f645ebbd7119 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/316040 Commit-Ready: Kalin Stoyanov &lt;kalin@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Kalin Stoyanov &lt;kalin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Sridhar Sonti &lt;sontis@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="316040" owner_email="kalin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/40/316040/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="Icaad0f3bf5f064edd1df36d66faa09f6960f4a0e" commit="5aa8df488c17c06e8c7971f4ebe8863281ca7f49" commit_message="CHROMIUM: ASoC: dw-hdmi-audio: Change the audio input clock FS factor to 64  dw_hdmi controller would calculate the exact input audio clock through the audio input clock FS factor and the audio sample rate. Math like:  audio_clock = sample_rate * input_clkfs_factor;  Actually i also can't understand why audio could work on most monitors with this 'big' mistake. For now the rockchip i2s code just hardcode the clock FS factor to 64, and haven't find a good way to let codec receive the 'dynamic' FS factor, so just hardcode again.  BUG=chrome-os-partner:47998 TEST=My YAMAHA AV Receiver could play pcm audio now.  Change-Id: Icaad0f3bf5f064edd1df36d66faa09f6960f4a0e Signed-off-by: Yakir Yang &lt;ykk@rock-chips.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/314998 Reviewed-by: Cheng-Yi Chiang &lt;cychiang@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="314998" owner_email="ykk@rock-chips.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/98/314998/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I5e3556b14524c3df14181e708ee8bd10fd53834d" commit="d946b77cfff7023e71903d5e6750770d6ad0ee13" commit_message="freetype: upgrade to 2.6.2 with one line patch.  Upgraded media-libs/freetype to version 2.6.2 on amd64, arm, x86 with one local patch to turn back ON 'stem darkening' for CFF.  This is done to make it safe for merging to the branch with the benefit of security hardening in 2.6.2. We may turn stem darkening off again along with hint-slight for our CFF fonts in fontconfig in the trunk after merge.  To have this local patch, freetype is now in chromiumos-overaly.   BUG=chromium:551028 TEST=`emerge-{BOARD} freetype` on x86-generic, daisy, link TEST=`cbuildbot --remote -g 315881 chromiumos-sdk       x86-alex-release daisy-release`  Change-Id: I5e3556b14524c3df14181e708ee8bd10fd53834d Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/315881 Commit-Ready: Jungshik Shin &lt;jshin@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Jungshik Shin &lt;jshin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="315881" owner_email="jshin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/81/315881/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Idbddf43df41af030c84fb7133e53086b2377aaf3" commit="34c289efa7a7cc3cd8d686136cf50dffe5e1aa47" commit_message="Remove old branches from chameleon test suites  This is to relax the weekend schedule for chameleon test suites - Removing ToT-1 and ToT-2 from weekly runs - Added ToT-2 to chameleon perbuild  suites schedule  BUG=chromium:560951 TEST=None  Change